Upgrading Code
**************

.[perex]
Moving to a newer Nette, Symfony, Laravel or PHP? DressCode rewrites your code to the new API of your libraries and to the new PHP version, in the style your code already has, and reports exactly what is left for you to do. Raise the versions in `composer.json` and run a single command. For a big jump, let DressCode build a plan: it measures the move first and then carries it out step by step, with Composer and your tests.


What It Looks Like
==================

This is how DressCode rewrote the code of a Nette 3 application that moved to `nette/utils` 4.0:

```php .[before]
if (!Strings::endsWith($file, '.jpg')) {
	return Image::fromString(Image::EMPTY_GIF);
}

$image->resize(300, 200, Image::FIT | Image::SHRINK_ONLY);
file_put_contents($this->file, Json::encode($products, Json::PRETTY));
```

```php .[after]
if (!\str_ends_with($file, '.jpg')) {
	return Image::fromString(Image::EmptyGIF);
}

$image->resize(300, 200, Image::OrSmaller | Image::ShrinkOnly);
file_put_contents($this->file, Json::encode($products, pretty: true));
```

The method `Strings::endsWith()`, which `nette/utils` 4 no longer has, gave way to the PHP function `str_ends_with()`, the constants of the class `Image` got their new names, and the flag `Json::PRETTY` became a named argument. You did not have to look up or rewrite any of it by hand.

How does DressCode know what changed between versions? From **upgrade data**: the packages `dresscode/upgrade-nette`, `upgrade-symfony`, `upgrade-laravel` and `upgrade-deegee` describe what was renamed, moved or removed in the libraries, and DressCode itself knows what PHP deprecated. Where there is no data, the `@deprecated` annotations in the code of the library serve instead. [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries] covers it in detail.


Lower Bounds Decide
===================

The API version targeted by automated rewrites is not determined by what happens to be installed in `vendor/`. The deciding factor is exclusively the constraints declared in `composer.json`. Code must function on every version allowed by your constraints, so it must be written for the lowest compatible release: using features from newer releases would break environments that resolve to the lower bound. Given `"nette/forms": "^3.2"`, code is rewritten to the 3.2 API, even if 3.3 is currently installed in `vendor/`. The PHP version is read from `require.php` the same way, see [PHP and Package Versions |configuration#PHP and Package Versions].

Upgrading code is therefore not about downloading a newer release into `vendor/`, but about **raising the lower bound**. That is why an [upgrade plan |upgrade-plan] raises constraints in `composer.json` before rewriting code for the new API.

Changes from higher versions that your constraints also allow are neither rewritten nor even reported. A rewrite to the newer API would break the code on the very lowest version it has to work with, and a test with the lowest dependencies (`composer update --prefer-lowest`) would catch that at once. With `"^3.2 || ^4.0"`, code is therefore rewritten to the 3.2 API, and the changes of 4.0 wait until you raise the lower bound. Your tests with the highest dependencies reveal what would break on a higher allowed version.

What the library retired and DressCode cannot rewrite on its own is reported along with the version that retired it:

```
Function `acme_format()` is deprecated: use `Acme\Formatter`, since acme/lib 3.2.
```

The keyword `deprecated` indicates that the currently installed package still contains the symbol, whereas `removed` warns that it is gone. A library required without a lower bound (`*`), or one present purely as an unconstrained transitive dependency, can resolve to any version. Neither its upgrade data nor the annotations in its code say anything then, since nobody knows which version the code has to work with. Such libraries are listed on the `Unbounded` line in the [`upgrade today` |#When You Have Already Raised the Versions] header, while packages installed at a higher release than their constraint's minimum appear under `Lowest`.

To inspect where upgrade data begins and where raising lower bounds would lead, run `dresscode config`:

/--pre .[terminal]
Packages   3 with upgrading data
      nette/application 3.2           upgrading/application.neon of dresscode/upgrade-nette, upgrading further to 3.2.1, 3.2.3, 3.2.9, 3.2.10, 3.3
      nette/component-model 3.2       upgrading/component-model.neon of dresscode/upgrade-nette, upgrading further to 4.0
      nette/utils 4.1                 upgrading/utils.neon of dresscode/upgrade-nette, upgrading further to 4.1.5
\--


Where DressCode Runs
====================

DressCode requires PHP 8.4 and modern versions of its own dependencies, including `nette/utils` 4.1. Consequently, it cannot be installed as a local `vendor/` dependency inside an application running on an older PHP or on Nette 3. Install it globally instead, alongside PHPStan and the upgrade package for your ecosystem:

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode dresscode/upgrade-nette phpstan/phpstan
```

Substitute `upgrade-nette` with `upgrade-symfony`, `upgrade-laravel`, or `upgrade-deegee` (for Dibi and Texy); see the [table of packages |upgrading-libraries#Packages for Ecosystems] for full coverage. Once an application is on the new versions and only wants to guard against deprecated APIs, DressCode can be added directly to the project as a standard development dependency (see [below |#Keeping the Old API from Coming Back]).


Two Ways to Upgrade
===================

There are two ways to carry out an upgrade:

- **An upgrade plan** suits a big jump, such as from Nette 3 to Nette 4. DressCode first measures how much work the move takes, then builds a plan in steps and carries it out: it rewrites the code, raises the versions in `composer.json` through Composer, and after each step it can run your tests and commit the changes. It needs a configuration, `dresscode.neon`; if the project does not have one yet, `dresscode init` measures it from the code. [The Upgrade Plan Step by Step |upgrade-plan] describes the whole procedure.
- **The command `upgrade today`** suits the case when you have already raised the versions in `composer.json` yourself: it rewrites the code to the API of the versions the project requires today, and it works even without a configuration. The next chapter describes it.

Every option of both ways is detailed in the [command line reference |cli#upgrade].


When You Have Already Raised the Versions
=========================================

If you have already updated constraints in `composer.json` yourself (using `composer require`, for example) and simply want to adapt your codebase to the new APIs your project now demands, run one command:

```shell
dresscode upgrade today
```

What `dresscode upgrade today` does:

- **Requires no configuration file.** It automatically inspects the entire project, deriving library and PHP targets directly from `composer.json` constraints (see [above |#Lower Bounds Decide]).
- **Rewrites outdated APIs exclusively.** It executes the [`deprecations` |php-coding-standards#deprecations] set, applying the data of installed upgrade packages with their rules and handling PHP engine deprecations. Adding `--modernize` also updates syntax to newer PHP constructs (the `modernizations` set).
- **Preserves existing visual style.** Without a configuration file, it enforces no formatting style; with a configuration file, it applies only the formatting decisions your codebase already satisfies (see [below |#Your Style Stays]).
- **Relies on static types from PHPStan**, without which method targets cannot be reliably identified. If PHPStan is not installed, the command refuses to run.
- **Reports remaining manual tasks**, and if edits were made, advises you to run it a second time.

Here is the terminal output from upgrading a Nette application:

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     /var/www/shop/dresscode.neon
Target     PHP 8.2 from `composer.json`
Upgrading  nette/application, nette/component-model, nette/forms, nette/http, nette/utils
Fixing     214 files in /var/www/shop

app/Presentation/Product/ProductPresenter.php  rewritten
app/Presentation/Sign/SignPresenter.php  rewritten
app/Model/Referrals.php
  error  27:26  Method `Nette\Http\Request::getReferer()` is forbidden: use `getOrigin()`, which reads the Origin header and gives only the scheme, the host and the port instead of the whole URL of the referring page.  upgrading.api.forbiddenMembers

FIXED  18 violations found, 1 remaining in 3 of 214 files, 3.2 s
Run `dresscode upgrade today` again: the code it rewrote may hold more to upgrade.
\--

The `Upgrading` line lists all libraries whose upgrade data was activated. Files marked `rewritten` were updated; remaining manual tasks are listed under their respective files. The final line suggests a second run because freshly rewritten code may expose secondary deprecations that the first run could not detect. A run that makes no edits omits this suggestion. How to guard against the old API in every check is described in [Keeping the Old API from Coming Back |#Keeping the Old API from Coming Back].


A Diff You Can Trust
--------------------

The `upgrade today` command writes changes directly to disk, letting Git present the resulting diff. Start with a clean working tree and inspect the diff after the run: you will find only locations where legacy APIs were used, rewritten to match surrounding conventions, complete with accurate imports. DressCode performs no naive string replacements. A method call is rewritten only where static analysis proves it belongs to that specific class: `getName()` is updated on `FileUpload` instances and left untouched everywhere else. See [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries] for architectural details. To start cautiously, target a single file first: `dresscode upgrade today --paths app/Model/Orders.php`.

Always review the diff. Upgrade data describes syntactic patterns, not runtime semantics: if a new method version returns `-1` instead of `null`, DressCode updates the method call but leaves an adjacent `=== null` comparison intact. After upgrading, always run your test suite and PHPStan.


Your Style Stays
----------------

A project without a `dresscode.neon` has no defined style, and `upgrade today` will touch only deprecated APIs. When a configuration exists, everything relevant to upgrading applies: `paths`, `excludePaths`, target PHP versions, `fixRisky`, baselines, `overrides`, and custom [replacement maps |upgrading-libraries#Your Own Renames in a Project]. For formatting decisions, however, it applies only those that your code already follows: DressCode tests your code against your configured style and drops any formatting decision that is violated anywhere in the project. Newly inserted code matches your real conventions, and modified files are spared unrelated reformatting.

The unit is the decision, and this filtering is project-wide: a single line exceeding length limits anywhere disables `file.longLines` across the entire upgrade run. If you want to reformat your codebase as well, run `dresscode fix` afterward, ideally as a separate, dedicated commit.


Missing Data
------------

The `upgrade` command discovers available upgrade packages from Packagist (currently covering packages under the official `dresscode` vendor). It queries at most once per day and caches responses locally, allowing it to work offline (if offline with an empty cache, it simply offers no new packages). The estimate highlights missing packages, and `upgrade today` prompts to install them, provided your project requires that library directly in `require` or `require-dev`:

/--pre .[terminal]
The libraries of the project have upgrading data in `dresscode/upgrade-nette`, which DressCode does not have.
Install it beside DressCode in `C:/Users/me/AppData/Roaming/Composer`?
\--

The package installs into your DressCode environment, leaving your project's `composer.json` untouched. If DressCode is installed as a direct project dependency (in `composer.lock`), the package is installed into the project via `composer require --dev`, and the prompt notes that `composer.json` will be modified. Outside an interactive terminal (in CI or AI agent hooks), it installs nothing, exits with code 3, and prints the exact Composer command; passing `--yes` approves installation non-interactively.


One Major Version at a Time, by Hand
------------------------------------

If you prefer not to use an automated plan, you can break a major version jump into manual steps, one major release at a time:

1. In `composer.json`, bump the lower bound to the next major version (e.g. from `"nette/utils": "^3.2"` to `"^4.0"`). If upgrading PHP as well, raise `require.php` and `config.platform.php` if defined.
2. Run `composer update --prefer-lowest` so `vendor/` contains the lowest supported versions that code must target at this stage.
3. Run `dresscode upgrade today` (and rerun if recommended).
4. Review remaining manual tasks, run tests and PHPStan, review the diff, and commit.
5. Proceed to the next version, or run a standard `composer update` to bring dependencies to their latest releases.

Why is `--prefer-lowest` critical? Where upgrade data is absent, DressCode rewrites code based on `@deprecated` annotations in library source code. However, annotations speak only for the specific version currently installed in `vendor/`. DressCode intentionally ignores annotations from packages installed above their constraint's lower bound, as proposed replacements might not exist in the minimum allowed release.

Stepping through major versions ensures upgrade data aligns with the library code present in `vendor/`. New classes introduced in that version are available, and `@deprecated` annotations reveal what will disappear in the subsequent release; DressCode uses them to rewrite the code right away, or at least to show where you will need to change it. [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries#One Version at a Time, or All at Once] details how to upgrade applications all at once or rewrite code before updating packages.


What Is Left to You
===================

A high-quality upgrade consists of automated rewrites paired with a list of remaining tasks, and that list represents half of its value. DressCode never guesses: where a clean automated replacement does not exist, or where a developer must choose it, it flags the location and provides instructions:

```
Constant `Nette\Application\IRouter::SECURED` is forbidden: write the protocol, `https://`, into the mask of the route.
Method `Nette\Http\UrlScript::setPath()` is forbidden: the class is immutable; take a changed copy with `withPath()`.
```

Every message is an actionable directive. You can work through the list manually, or hand it directly to an AI agent: `upgrade today -f agent` formats findings specifically for LLM consumption, while `--format json` outputs estimates, plans, runs, and tasks as structured data (see [AI Agents |ai-agents#Upgrading with an Agent]). DressCode also reports locations where upgrade data provides a fix that cannot safely be applied in context; the message states the reason, leaving the code untouched.

**Risky fixes** wait for explicit approval. Certain syntactic transformations can alter runtime behavior, such as reading a property on the left of `??`, where PHP first checks whether it is set. In a plan, these are categorized as [manual tasks |upgrade-plan#Manual Tasks Along the Way]. Decisions you trust globally can be approved via the [`fixRisky` |configuration#risky-fixes] configuration key, and running `dresscode upgrade today --ask-risky` lets you review and approve risky fixes one by one with inline diffs.

**What upgrade data cannot see.** Upgrade data describes API signatures, not runtime behavior shifts, template syntax, or NEON configuration files; consult official library upgrade guides for those areas. Furthermore, if a library removes something the data does not record, it will only be caught by PHPStan.


Keeping the Old API from Coming Back
====================================

An upgrade is a one-time cleanup. To ensure retired APIs don't creep back in, and to guard against regressions in [continuous integration |continuous-integration] and [Git hooks |git-hooks], add the `deprecations` set and type analysis to your configuration, listing the upgrade package in `use`:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- perCs           # your standard
	- deprecations
	- upgrade-nette
```

Official packages from the `dresscode` vendor can be written using their short names in `use` just like built-in sets (the full package name `dresscode/upgrade-nette` works as well). Upgrade data applies even without the package name in `use`; naming it enables the executable rules bundled with the package, such as converting Nette's `Json::PRETTY` flag to a named argument. If omitted from `use`, both `check` and `fix` will display a one-time reminder:

/--pre .[terminal]
Warning: Package `dresscode/upgrade-nette` brings rules that run only where `use` names it, as `upgrade-nette`, and in `dresscode upgrade`; skipped, with what its upgrading files set for them.
\--

From that point on, `dresscode check` flags legacy API usage in newly authored code. And because data tracks constraints in `composer.json`, the check will highlight necessary updates the moment you raise a dependency to the next version.


Upgrading a Library You Maintain
================================

Libraries that depend on third-party packages are upgraded similarly to applications, except their constraints are widened rather than raised, so the upgrade plan of a library is different (see [A Library Widens, It Does Not Raise |upgrade-plan#A Library Widens, It Does Not Raise]). When I upgraded `nette/application` this way, `upgrade today` updated `setFactory()` calls to `setCreator()` and converted `RouteList::ONE_WAY` flags to `oneWay: 1` across both source and test files, flagged a deprecated property and an annotation whose text does not fit into an attribute, and required no changes on the second run.

However, libraries have a distinct requirement: test suites that invoke deprecated APIs intentionally (such as backward compatibility tests, deprecated attribute tests, or type tests of interfaces). You do not want those rewritten. Exclude these test files using an [override |configuration#Different Values for Part of the Project] in `overrides`:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- nette
	- deprecations

overrides:
	-
		paths: [tests/UI/Requires.sameSite.handle.phpt]
		upgrading:
			packages: no
			declarations:
				deprecatedMember: keep
```

Setting `packages: no` removes package data for those files, together with the rewrites their files turn on, while `deprecatedMember: keep` disables rewrites driven by `@deprecated` annotations; code style and the remainder of the set remain fully active. For a single line, `// dresscode:ignore upgrading` is sufficient (see [Suppressions |suppressions-and-baseline]). Findings reported in these tests are expected: they point to old API that is there on purpose.

Internal implementation code inside a class is never rewritten by upgrade data, which describes how classes are consumed externally rather than how they are built internally. When `nette/application` data indicates that writes to `$request->post` should become `setPost($value)`, the line `$this->post = $post` inside `Request::setPost()` remains untouched; otherwise the method would recursively call itself. Subclasses, however, are external consumers and are upgraded normally. Upgrade data for magic properties (`__get()` and `__set()`) also applies to private property access from outside, since PHP routes external access to private properties through magic methods as well.


Upgrading in Stages
===================

To upgrade in stages (for example, upgrading Nette first and PHP later), specify a single target in your estimate and plan, or restrict `upgrade today` with `--only` to a single package and its rules:

```shell
dresscode upgrade today --only upgrade-nette
```

Data from other packages takes effect only if covered by another name in `--only` (such as `deprecations` or `upgrading.api`). You can filter down to a specific library: `--only nette/forms` isolates files related to `nette/forms` regardless of which package supplies them, and `--only php` targets PHP engine deprecations. The same `--only` option is supported by `check` and `fix`, and package/library names are accepted by `fixRisky` and `severity`.

.[note]
Naming a package whose data has no effect in the project triggers an error explaining the cause (either the data does not match the project's dependency versions, or `upgrading.packages` is not enabled). A name that represents both an upgrade package and a library described by another package covers both: the files in that package and files describing that library.


